(1002983, 116658, 8891, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by visaksen:</b></div><hr/><i> what is bizarre here ?<br/> <br/>villy </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Beautiful great, wonderful image. Amazing? Yes!!! Bizarre? hmmmm....not in the least.  Really like it! Congrats!</td>', 'Originally posted by visaksen: what is bizarre here ? villy   Beautiful great, wonderful image. Amazing? Yes!!! Bizarre? hmmmm....not in the least.  Really like it! Congrats!', '2004-11-10 07:35:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(1002990, 116932, 2764, '<td valign="top">Our pumpkins suffered the same fate - when the squirrels had done the hard work, the chipmunks snuck inside, stuffed their cheeks with the seeds, and scooted.  This HAS to be the origin of the pumpkin carving tradition.  No doubt about it!   Since i have had chickens I have figured out the origin of the easter egg hunt.  In the spring, the chickens start laying their eggs outside, instead of in the hen house.  They hide them everywhere and if you want breakfast (or dont want skunks) you send the kids out to look for them.<br/> <br/>Great picture!</td>', 'Our pumpkins suffered the same fate - when the squirrels had done the hard work, the chipmunks snuck inside, stuffed their cheeks with the seeds, and scooted.  This HAS to be the origin of the pumpkin carving tradition.  No doubt about it!   Since i have had chickens I have figured out the origin of the easter egg hunt.  In the spring, the chickens start laying their eggs outside, instead of in the hen house.  They hide them everywhere and if you want breakfast (or dont want skunks) you send the kids out to look for them. Great picture!', '2004-11-10 07:46:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(1003024, 117370, 28505, '<td valign="top">Thank you both for your comments. It was my first entry and done at the last min. I know, no excuse. I am here to learn as much as I can so any and all comments mean alot to me. Thanks again.</td>', 'Thank you both for your comments. It was my first entry and done at the last min. I know, no excuse. I am here to learn as much as I can so any and all comments mean alot to me. Thanks again.', '2004-11-10 08:10:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(1003041, 116512, 8005, '<td valign="top">Great job! I think it''s funny that people thought you faked the color. Of course who would think that this sort of display could be natural. I cannot believe that anyone gave you scores under a 5. I am very proud of you.</td>', 'Great job! I think it''s funny that people thought you faked the color. Of course who would think that this sort of display could be natural. I cannot believe that anyone gave you scores under a 5. I am very proud of you.', '2004-11-10 08:14:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(1003155, 116165, 24511, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by StormyWeathers:</b></div><hr/><i> How do you take pictures like this in the night? I tried a few night shots but all my shots came back blurry. This is a nice shot. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This was my second attempt at a night photo and my first with a unfamiliar camera.  I followed the instructions from the borrowed canon G5.  Tripod, 3 seconds timer, Slow Synchro on, slow shutter speed and flash on.  I was getting tree leaves in the forground but opted for the simple moon and space framing for my entry.  I agree with previous comments that the moon is still out of focus but using a tripod helps a lot to avoid shakiness.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-11-10 08:49:26.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by StormyWeathers: How do you take pictures like this in the night? I tried a few night shots but all my shots came back blurry. This is a nice shot.   This was my second attempt at a night photo and my first with a unfamiliar camera.  I followed the instructions from the borrowed canon G5.  Tripod, 3 seconds timer, Slow Synchro on, slow shutter speed and flash on.  I was getting tree leaves in the forground but opted for the simple moon and space framing for my entry.  I agree with previous comments that the moon is still out of focus but using a tripod helps a lot to avoid shakiness.', '2004-11-10 08:48:26.000000', '2004-11-10 08:49:26.000000', 0),
(1003227, 117382, 15497, '<td valign="top">Funny :-)<br/></td>', 'Funny :-)', '2004-11-10 09:12:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(1003324, 117679, 23141, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by moswyn:</b></div><hr/><i> Wonderful colors, but the focus doesn''t seem to be right. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Hi jazper - you emailed me about the focusing...  To follow up, it looks as though your camera is focused more on the leaves of the plant and not the actual flower itself.  Hope that helps!</td>', 'Originally posted by moswyn: Wonderful colors, but the focus doesn''t seem to be right.   Hi jazper - you emailed me about the focusing...  To follow up, it looks as though your camera is focused more on the leaves of the plant and not the actual flower itself.  Hope that helps!', '2004-11-10 09:37:55.000000', NULL, 0),
